# General rules — Clean Logic project

## Communication

- Respond in Russian by default. The user's primary language is Russian.
- Default to brief, to-the-point answers. Be verbose only when the user explicitly asks for detail or when introducing a new technical concept for the first time.
- The user is a non-developer (digital marketer learning to code). Explain technical terms in plain language with analogies whenever you introduce a new concept.
- Never use jargon without immediately defining it.
- Use markdown formatting for structure (headings, lists, code blocks), but do not overuse emojis. No emojis unless the user explicitly requests them.

## Licensing constraints (STRICT)

Use only libraries, fonts, icons, and code with permissive licenses:

- MIT
- Apache 2.0
- BSD (any variant)
- ISC
- MPL 2.0
- OFL (for fonts)
- CC0 or CC-BY (for assets, attribution allowed)

Never use:

- GPL, AGPL, LGPL (copyleft — forbidden)
- SSPL
- Any paid or proprietary library
- Any asset with unclear or commercial-restricted licensing

These constraints are absolute. If a requirement seems to require a forbidden license, propose an alternative.

## Code style

- Do not add narrative comments that describe what the code does line by line.
- Avoid obvious comments like "// Import the module", "// Increment the counter", "// Return the result".
- Comments are reserved for non-obvious intent, trade-offs, or constraints.
- Never explain your edit inside the code as a comment.
- Prefer readable variable names over comments where possible.

## Project context

`AGENTS.md` in the project root is the navigation index for all documentation. Read it first when unsure where to look.

Before starting any non-trivial task, read the relevant documents from `docs/`:

- `docs/business/PROJECT_OVERVIEW.md` — what the project is, business context, legal context.
- `docs/business/BUSINESS_GOALS.md` — current and future goals.
- `docs/technical/TECH_STACK.md` — chosen stack and rationale.
- `docs/technical/MIGRATION_PLAN.md` — phases of the Tilda-to-Astro migration.

For rules specific to the Tilda migration, see `.cursor/rules/tilda-migration.mdc`.
For workflow conventions (bug reporting, destructive actions), see `.cursor/rules/workflow.mdc`.
For anti-complexity and transparency, see `.cursor/rules/simplicity.mdc`.
For design work, see `.cursor/rules/design.mdc`. For copy, see `.cursor/rules/copywriting.mdc`.
